            <ArticleTitle>A snakebite caused by a bushmaster (Lachesis muta): report of a confirmed case in State of Pernambuco, Brazil.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>636-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1590/0037-8682-0143-2015</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0037-86822015000500636</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We report a case of envenomation caused by a bushmaster ( Lachesis muta) in a male child in State of Pernambuco, Brazil. The victim showed discrete local manifestations, but presented altered blood coagulation 2 hours after the bite. Ten ampoules of bothropic-lachetic antivenom therapy were administered, and 48 hours later, the patient showed discrete edema, pain, and ecchymosis around the bite and normal blood coagulation. The patient was discharged 5 days after the envenomation. The prompt administration of specific treatment was important for the favorable outcomes observed.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
